        {
            "id": "33702815",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "00:56, 29 September 2025",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,520 bytes)",
            "change": -355,
            "summary": "(Removed and replaced bad sources for wikiHaus content team)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Obtain the necessary building permits. Your city will likely require you to have a residential building permit in order to put a swimming pool on your property. You can usually apply for a residential building permit on the website of your local Department of Building and Safety. [[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pick out a suitable site for the pool. Designate a part of your property that would work best for your new pool. Swimming pools are typically found in the backyards of most homes, though you could also situate yours to one side if you have enough space to play with.<ref>https://home.howstuffworks.com/swimming-pool.htm</ref>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 2 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase a molded fiberglass pool for ease of installation. Fiberglass pools are most often sold as one-piece premade forms. This make them a breeze to put in, as all the building crew has to do after excavating your site is lower them into place and backfill around them. Fiberglass also offers the best balance of durability and affordability. [[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 5 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "*In some places, swimming pools and similar projects are considered taxable home additions. The exception to this is if the finished pool doesn’t increase your home’s property value.<ref>https://www.austlii.edu.au/cgi-bin/viewdoc/au/legis/cth/num_act/sptra1992296/s4.html</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Obtain the necessary building permits. Your city will likely require you to have a residential building permit in order to put a swimming pool on your property. You can usually apply for a residential building permit on the website of your local Department of Building and Safety.<ref>https://www.ladbs.org/docs/default-source/publications/information-bulletins/building-code/design-and-construction-of-swimming-pools-ib-p-bc2017-014.pdf?sfvrsn=22</ref>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Pick out a suitable site for the pool. Designate a part of your property that would work best for your new pool. Swimming pools are typically found in the backyards of most homes, though you could also situate yours to one side if you have enough space to play with.<ref>https://www.popularmechanics.com/home/outdoor-projects/how-to/a3482/4276117/</ref>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 2 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Purchase a molded fiberglass pool for ease of installation. Fiberglass pools are most often sold as one-piece premade forms. This make them a breeze to put in, as all the building crew has to do after excavating your site is lower them into place and backfill around them. Fiberglass also offers the best balance of durability and affordability.<ref>https://www.angieslist.com/articles/are-fiberglass-pools-better-concrete.htm</ref>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 5 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "*In some places, swimming pools and similar projects are considered taxable home additions. The exception to this is if the finished pool doesn’t increase your home’s property value.<ref>https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/get-there/wp/2016/02/19/swimming-pools-gas-money-and-other-expenses-you-didnt-know-could-be-tax-breaks/?noredirect=on</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "33368781",
            "user": "WikiHow Projects",
            "timestamp": "18:23, 2 May 2025",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,875 bytes)",
            "change": 74,
            "summary": "(Adding internal links)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Think carefully about where your pool will go. Installing a swimming pool in certain areas may require you to add or remove other outdoor features like [[Remove-a-Tree|trees]] or sections of fencing, which will drive up the cost of the project.",
                    "#Create the foundation of your pool with concrete to ensure that it's built to last. Most residential swimming pools are finished using 1 of 3 surfacing materials: poured concrete, fiberglass, or vinyl. [[Build-a-Concrete-Pool|Concrete]] is the strongest and most durable, but also the most expensive. It tends to be the best choice for large pools that are intended as a permanent addition to a home's exterior.<ref>https://www.emol.org/homeimprovement/swimmingpools/poolmaterialtypes.html</ref>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Properly Maintain Swimming Pool Water Chemistry|Treat your pool]] with the necessary chemicals. Use a home water testing kit to [[Measure-the-pH-of-Water|find the pH of the water]] in your pool. Then, add chlorine and other chemicals like shock and algaecide as needed to bring it to the recommended levels for safe swimming. The ideal pH for the water in a swimming pool is 7.4-7.6, or just above neutral.[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 17 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The exact amount of [[Chlorinate-a-Pool|chlorine]] you'll need will depend on the volume of your pool. If you're unsure how much to add, contact a pool maintenance company for assistance.",
                    "*After digging up the ground, you have to follow proper ways of disposing soil and other materials that you may find."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Think carefully about where your pool will go. Installing a swimming pool in certain areas may require you to add or remove other outdoor features like trees or sections of fencing, which will drive up the cost of the project.",
                    "#Create the foundation of your pool with concrete to ensure that it's built to last. Most residential swimming pools are finished using 1 of 3 surfacing materials: poured concrete, fiberglass, or vinyl. Concrete is the strongest and most durable, but also the most expensive. It tends to be the best choice for large pools that are intended as a permanent addition to a home's exterior.<ref>https://www.emol.org/homeimprovement/swimmingpools/poolmaterialtypes.html</ref>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 5.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Properly Maintain Swimming Pool Water Chemistry|Treat your pool]] with the necessary chemicals. Use a home water testing kit to find the pH of the water in your pool. Then, add chlorine and other chemicals like shock and algaecide as needed to bring it to the recommended levels for safe swimming. The ideal pH for the water in a swimming pool is 7.4-7.6, or just above neutral.[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 17 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*The exact amount of chlorine you'll need will depend on the volume of your pool. If you're unsure how much to add, contact a pool maintenance company for assistance.",
                    "*After digging up the ground, you have to follow proper ways of [[Dispose of Soil|disposing soil]] and other materials that you may find."
                ]
            }
        },
