            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "reverted_by": "GB742",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*Always keep your pool covered in the off-season when you’re not using it. This will both keep it clean and protect it from damage as a result of exposure to the elements.",
                    "*Once your pool is finished, it will be up to you to see to the cleaning, chlorination, and general upkeep, unless you elect to hire a professional pool service."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "*Once your pool is finished, it will be up to you to see to the cleaning, chlorination, and general upkeep, unless you elect to hire a professional pool service.",
                    "*Always keep your pool covered in the off-season when you’re not using it. This will both keep it clean and protect it from damage as a result of exposure to the elements."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "24864309",
            "user": "Sophia B",
            "timestamp": "16:51, 6 July 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(18,340 bytes)",
            "change": 25,
            "summary": "(Grammatical edit.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Create the foundation of your pool with concrete to ensure that it's built to last. Most residential swimming pools are finished using 1 of 3 surfacing materials: poured concrete, fiberglass, or vinyl. Concrete is the strongest and most durable, but also the most expensive. It tends to be the best choice for large pools that are intended as a permanent addition to a home's exterior.<ref>https://www.emol.org/homeimprovement/swimmingpools/poolmaterialtypes.html</ref>",
                    "#*The exact amount of chlorine you'll need will depend on the volume of your pool. If you're unsure how much to add, contact a pool maintenance company for assistance.",
                    "",
                    "<!-- Quick Summary code below. Please move/edit via section Edit link if needed. -->",
                    "== Quick Summary ==",
                    "{{Summary:Build-a-Swimming-Pool}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "<!-- Quick Summary code below. Please move/edit via section Edit link if needed. -->",
                    "== Quick Summary ==",
                    "{{Summary:Build-a-Swimming-Pool}}",
                    "#Surface your pool with concrete to ensure that it's built to last. Most residential swimming pools are finished using 1 of 3 surfacing materials: poured concrete, fiberglass, or vinyl. Concrete is the strongest and most durable, but also the most expensive. It tends to be the best choice for large pools that are intended as a permanent addition to a home's exterior.<ref>https://www.emol.org/homeimprovement/swimmingpools/poolmaterialtypes.html</ref>",
                    "#*The exact amount of chlorine you'll need will depend on the volume of your pool. If you're unsure how much to add, contact a pool car company for assistance."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "24861275",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "22:44, 5 July 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(18,315 bytes)",
            "change": 3340,
            "summary": "(Added steps on each of the main 3 surfacing materials, as well as hiring a pool contractor and treating the water in the pool once it's been filled; included clarifying detail where specified; replaced content marketing sources)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#*Think carefully about where your pool will go. Installing a swimming pool in certain areas may require you to add or remove other outdoor features like trees or sections of fencing, which will drive up the cost of the project.",
                    "#Come up with a basic design for your pool. Think about how you want your new pool to look. Would you prefer a round, square, or rectangular shape? How deep will it be? How many entrances and exits will it have? These are all important details that you'll need to discuss with your contractor before the project gets underway.<ref>https://www.housebeautiful.com/room-decorating/outdoor-ideas/g1416/pool-design-ideas/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Most home swimming pools come in standardized sizes like {{convert|10|ft|m}} x {{convert|20|ft|m}}, {{convert|15|ft|m}} x {{convert|30|ft|m}}, and {{convert|20|ft|m}} x {{convert|40|ft|m}}, and may be anywhere from {{convert|4-15|ft|m}} deep.<ref>https://www.poolpricer.com/inground-pool-sizes/</ref>",
                    "#Surface your pool with concrete to ensure that it's built to last. Most residential swimming pools are finished using 1 of 3 surfacing materials: poured concrete, fiberglass, or vinyl. Concrete is the strongest and most durable, but also the most expensive. It tends to be the best choice for large pools that are intended as a permanent addition to a home's exterior.<ref>https://www.emol.org/homeimprovement/swimmingpools/poolmaterialtypes.html</ref>",
                    "#*If you expect your pool to receive heavy use, investing in a long-lasting material like concrete can cut down on the cost of repairs in the long run.",
                    "#*A well-maintained concrete swimming pool can also add to the resale value of your home, in some cases.",
                    "#Purchase a molded fiberglass pool for ease of installation. Fiberglass pools are most often sold as one-piece premade forms. This make them a breeze to put in, as all the building crew has to do after excavating your site is lower them into place and backfill around them. Fiberglass also offers the best balance of durability and affordability.<ref>https://www.angieslist.com/articles/are-fiberglass-pools-better-concrete.htm</ref>",
                    "#*For saltwater pools, you’ll want to go with fiberglass or vinyl lining, as salt can wear down concrete over time.<ref>https://household-tips.thefuntimesguide.com/salt_water_swimming_pools/</ref>",
                    "#Go with a vinyl-lined pool if you want to spend less. Vinyl swimming pool liners are essentially large, smooth sheets that are shaped to fit the contours of the completed pool. They're by far the most economical option for finishing a home swimming pool. The major downside is that they wear out much faster than both fiberglass and concrete.<ref>http://www.foxnews.com/real-estate/2012/06/30/swimming-pools-101-what-to-think-about-if-re-considering-pool.html</ref>",
                    "#*Another disadvantage of vinyl pool liners is that they have a tendency to bubble, wrinkle, and pull loose. This means they may need regular adjusting in order to keep their attractive appearance.",
                    "#*A vinyl-lined pool will only run you $25,000-50,000 on average, compared to poured concrete, which can cost upwards of $80,000-100,000.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kvUOpD541hQ&feature=youtu.be&t=73</ref>",
                    "#Hire a pool contractor to bring your vision to life. Run a search for pool contractor companies in your area and compare your options carefully. You'll want to select a professional who holds a valid contractor's license signifying that they're qualified to install swimming pools. Experience building pools for at least several years is also a plus.<ref>http://www.cslb.ca.gov/Consumers/Hire_A_Contractor/Swimming_Pool_Construction/Selecting_A_Pool_Contractor.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*Read reviews from homeowners who have used the contractor in the past to get a sense of what kind of work they do.",
                    "#*The contractor you hire to put in your pool will charge a separate rate for labor, so don't forget to factor their pay into your initial budget.<ref>https://www.homewyse.com/service_pros/swimming_pool_contractor.html</ref>",
                    "#Grade the ground at the bottom of the hole. Once the site has been excavated, the next step is to even out what will become the floor of the pool as much as possible. This will involve covering the bottom surface with a thick layer of washed gravel subsoil, which is then raked and compressed until it’s perfectly level.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Have fiberglass pools set professionally. Most fiberglass pools come in a single-piece construction. If you picked out a premade fiberglass pool for your yard, it will need to be put in place using a crane or similar piece of equipment. Afterwards, the building crew will fill in the space around the pool with dirt or concrete.<ref>https://www.popularmechanics.com/home/outdoor-projects/how-to/a3482/4276117/</ref>",
                    "#[[Properly Maintain Swimming Pool Water Chemistry|Treat your pool]] with the necessary chemicals. Use a home water testing kit to find the pH of the water in your pool. Then, add chlorine and other chemicals like shock and algaecide as needed to bring it to the recommended levels for safe swimming. The ideal pH for the water in a swimming pool is 7.4-7.6, or just above neutral.<ref>http://www.maintainyourpool.com/pool-water-maintenance-tips.html</ref>",
                    "#*You can pick up a water testing kit anywhere that sells pool supplies.",
                    "#*The exact amount of chlorine you'll need will depend on the volume of your pool. If you're unsure how much to add, contact a pool car company for assistance.",
                    "#*If you're planning on having a salt water pool, you'll add the salt (along with the correct proportions of other chemicals) after the pool has been filled.<ref>http://pooluniversity.org/how-much-salt-to-add-to-pool/</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#*Think carefully about where your pool will go. Installing a swimming pool in certain areas may require you to add or remove other outdoor features, which will drive up the cost of the project.",
                    "#Come up with a basic design for your pool. Think about how you want your new pool to look. Would you prefer a round, square, or rectangular shape? How deep will it be? How many entrances and exits will it have? These are all details that you’ll need to communicate to your building crew once the project gets underway.<ref>https://www.housebeautiful.com/room-decorating/outdoor-ideas/g1416/pool-design-ideas/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Choose your preferred surfacing material. Most residential swimming pools are built using 1 of 3 materials: poured concrete, fiberglass, or vinyl. Concrete is the strongest and most durable, but also the most expensive. Fiberglass liners provide a good balance of durability and affordability. Vinyl liners are the most economical option, but tend to wear out faster than fiberglass and concrete.<ref>https://www.emol.org/homeimprovement/swimmingpools/poolmaterialtypes.html</ref>",
                    "#*If you think your pool will receive heavy use, it may be a good idea to invest in a longer-lasting material like fiberglass or concrete to cut down on patches and repairs.",
                    "#*For saltwater pools, you’ll want to go with fiberglass or vinyl lining, as salt can wear down concrete over time.<ref>https://www.riverpoolsandspas.com/fiberglass-vs-concrete-vs-vinyl-liner-inground-pools</ref>",
                    "#Grade the ground at the bottom of the hole. Once the site has been excavated, the next step is to even out what will become the floor of the pool as much as possible. This will involve covering the bottom surface with a thick layer of washed gravel subsoil, which is then raked and compressed until it’s perfectly level.<ref>https://www.compasspools.eu/advices/how-to-build-a-swimming-pool-step-by-step/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Have fiberglass pools set professionally. Most fiberglass pools come in a single-piece construction. If you picked out a premade fiberglass pool for your yard, it will need to be put in place using a crane or similar piece of equipment. Afterwards, the building crew will fill in the space around the pool with dirt or concrete.<ref>https://www.riverpoolsandspas.com/fiberglass-pool-installation-process</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "24848554",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "17:45, 3 July 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,975 bytes)",
            "change": 7214,
            "summary": "(Organized article into Parts dealing with the different phases of construction; revised content to make steps more actionable; included important supplementary info and suggestions; cited new sources)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "A swimming pool can make a great addition to a big back yard, but installing one is far from a DIY project. In most cities, you won’t be allowed to build a pool yourself, as construction must be supervised by a licensed builder in order to be considered up to code. However, the installation process is similar, whether you’re putting in a fiberglass, vinyl, or concrete pool. Start by choosing a suitable location for your new pool, then deciding on your preferred shape and depth. You’ll then hire out a building crew, who will excavate the area, add the necessary plumbing and electrical wiring elements, and finally pour concrete or insert a water-retentive liner before filling the pool.",
                    "",
                    "<!-- Quick Summary code below. Please move/edit via section Edit link if needed. -->",
                    "== Quick Summary ==",
                    "{{Summary:Build-a-Swimming-Pool}}",
                    "",
                    "=== Planning Your Pool ===",
                    "#Obtain the necessary building permits. Your city will likely require you to have a residential building permit in order to put a swimming pool on your property. You can usually apply for a residential building permit on the website of your local Department of Building and Safety.<ref>https://www.ladbs.org/docs/default-source/publications/information-bulletins/building-code/design-and-construction-of-swimming-pools-ib-p-bc2017-014.pdf?sfvrsn=22</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*In addition to granting you permission to construct a swimming pool, the residential building permit will outline the exact requirements and specifications of your new pool.<ref>https://www.dos.ny.gov/dcea/pdf/PoolsumUC0708.pdf</ref>",
                    "#*Don’t forget to also check your neighborhood’s homeowners association guidelines before moving forward with the installation—not all neighborhoods allow pools.",
                    "#*Some municipalities may also expect you to have a separate electrical permit. However, the electrician you contract to take care of your pool’s inground wiring can often apply for an electrical permit on your behalf.",
                    "#Pick out a suitable site for the pool. Designate a part of your property that would work best for your new pool. Swimming pools are typically found in the backyards of most homes, though you could also situate yours to one side if you have enough space to play with.<ref>https://www.popularmechanics.com/home/outdoor-projects/how-to/a3482/4276117/</ref>",
                    "#*Think carefully about where your pool will go. Installing a swimming pool in certain areas may require you to add or remove other outdoor features, which will drive up the cost of the project.",
                    "#*You may have a limited number of options in regards to your pool’s overall dimensions, depending on where you decide to put it.<ref>https://www.bhg.com/home-improvement/outdoor/pools-spas/planning-for-a-pool/</ref>",
                    "#Come up with a basic design for your pool. Think about how you want your new pool to look. Would you prefer a round, square, or rectangular shape? How deep will it be? How many entrances and exits will it have? These are all details that you’ll need to communicate to your building crew once the project gets underway.<ref>https://www.housebeautiful.com/room-decorating/outdoor-ideas/g1416/pool-design-ideas/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Keep in mind that the more complex your pool design is, the higher the total cost will be.",
                    "#Choose your preferred surfacing material. Most residential swimming pools are built using 1 of 3 materials: poured concrete, fiberglass, or vinyl. Concrete is the strongest and most durable, but also the most expensive. Fiberglass liners provide a good balance of durability and affordability. Vinyl liners are the most economical option, but tend to wear out faster than fiberglass and concrete.<ref>https://www.emol.org/homeimprovement/swimmingpools/poolmaterialtypes.html</ref>",
                    "#*If you think your pool will receive heavy use, it may be a good idea to invest in a longer-lasting material like fiberglass or concrete to cut down on patches and repairs.",
                    "#*For saltwater pools, you’ll want to go with fiberglass or vinyl lining, as salt can wear down concrete over time.<ref>https://www.riverpoolsandspas.com/fiberglass-vs-concrete-vs-vinyl-liner-inground-pools</ref>",
                    "=== Creating the Basic Structure ===",
                    "#Excavate the area. Once you’ve hired a certified pool contractor, they’ll begin by marking off and digging up the ground on the site you’ve selected. To do this, they may need to use a backhoe and other heavy machinery. Excavation is one of the most important parts of the building process, as proper installation of other the pool’s other components depends on it being done right.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O_xvCbwyoaQ&feature=youtu.be&t=30</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*The building crew make ask for your help making your property accessible to their equipment.",
                    "#*It’s critical that you have your residential building permit on file at this point. Otherwise, you run the risk of damaging or unearthing utility lines you didn't know about.",
                    "#Grade the ground at the bottom of the hole. Once the site has been excavated, the next step is to even out what will become the floor of the pool as much as possible. This will involve covering the bottom surface with a thick layer of washed gravel subsoil, which is then raked and compressed until it’s perfectly level.<ref>https://www.compasspools.eu/advices/how-to-build-a-swimming-pool-step-by-step/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*There are a few different ways to grade a swimming pool. Some pools have flat bottoms, while others are built with sloping floors that establish a deep end and a shallow end. Be sure to tell your contractor which style you have in mind.",
                    "#Frame the walls with steel supports. With the ground dug up and the bottom surface graded, you’re now ready to begin forming the sides of the pool. The building crew will go around the circumference of the hole setting metal rebar and wooden boards. These materials are used to create shape and provide support.<ref>https://www.todayshomeowner.com/video/digging-and-pouring-a-backyard-swimming-pool/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Like the floor, it’s important that the walls of your new pool be flat, level, and in alignment with one another.",
                    "#*It may be necessary to hire a separate steel subcontractor to frame the walls of your pool if your building contractor isn’t licensed or equipped to do so.",
                    "#Install all necessary plumbing fixtures. At this point, you’ll need to bring in a licensed plumbing expert to set up the pool’s water circulation and filtration systems. This will include components like the drain, skimmers, 2- and 3-way valves, and the main pump that are responsible for filling, emptying, and moving water throughout the pool.<ref>https://www.popularmechanics.com/home/outdoor-projects/how-to/a3482/4276117/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 6.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 6.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Be sure to hire someone with extensive experience working on pools—an inexperienced plumber could make a mess of your new pool before it’s even finished.",
                    "#*Note that plumbing systems may differ depending on the building codes where you live.<ref>https://codes.iccsafe.org/public/document/ISPSC2015/chapter-3-general-compliance</ref>",
                    "#Wire the pool for electricity. Before the pool is lined, an electrical contractor will need to run power to your in order to power the filtration system and any underwater lighting elements you wish to include. This is another time when it’s important to secure the services of a professional, as bad wiring and water can be a dangerous combination.<ref>http://www.floridabuilding.org/fbc/thecode/2017_Code_Development/2017_Resources/Swimming%20Pool%20Electrical%20Safety%20-%20fact%20sheet%20-%202016-06-30.pdf</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 7.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 7.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Ask your main building contractor for recommendations for trustworthy electricians that they’ve worked with in the past.",
                    "#*Swimming pool electrical safety is a major concern. Faulty wiring could increase swimmers’ risk of electrical shock.<ref>https://www.cpsc.gov/safety-education/safety-guides/pools-and-spas/dont-swim-shocks-electrical-safety-and-around-pools</ref>",
                    "=== Lining the Pool ===",
                    "#Pour the walls and floor if you’re putting in a concrete pool. With the plumbing and electrical fixtures in place, the building crew will use a concrete mixer to cover the steel forms set up along the interior of the pool. They’ll grade and smooth the concrete manually while it’s still wet.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yX9WNhuAArY&feature=youtu.be&t=18</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 8.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 8.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*For pools with varying depths, it will be necessary to check that the angle of slope is consistent from one end to the other.",
                    "#*The walls of concrete pools are typically finished with either cinder block or sprayed concrete. Take some time to discuss the benefits of each option with your building contractor.<ref>https://www.todayshomeowner.com/video/digging-and-pouring-a-backyard-swimming-pool/</ref>",
                    "#Have fiberglass pools set professionally. Most fiberglass pools come in a single-piece construction. If you picked out a premade fiberglass pool for your yard, it will need to be put in place using a crane or similar piece of equipment. Afterwards, the building crew will fill in the space around the pool with dirt or concrete.<ref>https://www.riverpoolsandspas.com/fiberglass-pool-installation-process</ref>",
                    "#*In some cases, fiberglass liners are installed by spraying the fiberglass material onto an existing frame rather than setting a premade pool in one piece.<ref>https://www.compositesworld.com/articles/swimming-pools-fiberglass-resurfacing-option-extends-useful-life</ref>",
                    "#Fit vinyl pools with a flexible liner. Vinyl liners can only be installed after the concrete base has been poured and given 1-2 full days to dry. The installers will unroll the one-piece vinyl sheet and stretch it out to cover the entire inner surface of the pool. They’ll then attach it by securing the built-in liner tack around the outer edges and insert a vacuum nozzle to suction out the air between the liner and the walls of the pool.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bozVrlYaZHY&feature=youtu.be&t=194</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Before installing the liner, the building crew will need to mark and cut out holes for the lights, skimmers, and any other fixtures on the pool’s interior.<ref>http://blog.intheswim.com/5-step-inground-pool-vinyl-liner-installation/</ref>",
                    "#*Vinyl liners are often easy enough to put in yourself, although having the assistance of a professional will ensure that installation is carried out correctly and efficiently.",
                    "#Fill up the pool. With the construction complete, all that’s left to do is add water! Depending on how eager you are to take your first dip, you could run the water slowly using a hose connected to your home’s water supply, or rent a water truck to top it off in less time.<ref> https://www.poolpricer.com/cost-to-fill-pool-with-water/</ref><br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12 Version 2.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Filling a swimming pool with a single water hose can be an all day (maybe even all night) event. When you rent a water truck, the water will be dispensed into your pool via multiple hoses to fill it much faster.",
                    "#*While filling your new pool, [[Find a Leak in Your Swimming Pool|check for leaks]], plumbing malfunctions, or other complications. If you discover an issue, contact the building contractor to schedule repairs before you use the pool.",
                    "*Take the time to research the various lining, plumbing, and filtration options you have available to you before finalizing the plans for your new pool.",
                    "*Consider taking additional safety measures beyond what’s laid out in your local building codes. For instance, it may be a good idea to build an enclosure around your pool if you have small children or pets, or make use of buoy ropes, multiple ladders, or other features for those who aren’t strong swimmers.",
                    "*Once your pool is finished, it will be up to you to see to the cleaning, chlorination, and general upkeep, unless you elect to hire a professional pool service.",
                    "*Always keep your pool covered in the off-season when you’re not using it. This will both keep it clean and protect it from damage as a result of exposure to the elements.",
                    "*In some places, swimming pools and similar projects are considered taxable home additions. The exception to this is if the finished pool doesn’t increase your home’s property value.<ref>https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/get-there/wp/2016/02/19/swimming-pools-gas-money-and-other-expenses-you-didnt-know-could-be-tax-breaks/?noredirect=on&utm_term=.ba09f9455fe8</ref>",
                    "== Sources and Citations ==",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "",
                    "__Parts__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Swimming pools are a great addition to a big back yard, creating a great way to spend time together as a family and get in some super fun exercise! It isn't the easiest building project, however. The average cost of a swimming pool, even if you're building it yourself, is about $25,000. Keep in mind that in most cities, you will not be allowed to build the pool yourself as you need to be a certified and licensed builder in order for the pool to be considered up to code. With these things in mind, get started with Step 1 to see what you can expect from the building process.",
                    "#Design the pool. You'll want to start by designing the pool that you want to build. Will it be round? Square? How deep will it be? What color do you want the pool to be? The more complex the design, the higher the cost.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Get a permit. Get a contractor lined up and apply for a permit with your local city. You might also have to apply with your local home owner's association, as not all neighborhoods allow pools. Your contractor may be able to do both of these things for you, if they are an experienced and qualified service.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 2.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 2.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*Keep in mind that in some areas, pools are taxed extra and you could get in a lot of trouble for failing to register your pool with the city. It can be viewed as tax evasion.",
                    "#Excavate the area. Rent the necessary construction equipment (usually at least a backhoe) and dig out the area where the pool will go. It is important to have your permit from the city at this point, as you don't want to risk running in to utility lines you didn't know about.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 3.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 3.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Grade the ground. You'll want to even out the ground as much as possible along what will become the bottom of the pool. This will make building the walls and putting in the floor much easier. There are many ways to grade the ground but if you are using a sloping floor, you'll definitely want someone with experience to do this.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Frame the walls. With the hole excavated and the land graded, you're ready to start putting in the walls. Start by framing the walls with wood and metal rebar. Make sure to keep the walls even and in line with each other.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 5.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 5.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Put in the plumbing. Get a licensed plumber to come in and add in the necessary plumbing for the pool. You will need to create a supply and filtration system which meets the codes for your area. Only a plumber with pool experience should be hired as an inexperienced one can ruin your pool.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 6.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 6.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Run the electricity. Get an electrician to run power to your pool if it is needed for lights or the filtration system. This is another time where it is important to hire a professional, as you can imagine what bad wiring plus water leads to.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 7.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 7.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Pour the floor. With the plumbing and electricity roughed in, you can start building the actual walls and floor. Get a cement truck and pour the floor, grading and smoothing once it is poured. Make sure to pay attention to angle of the floor if making a pool with depth variation.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 8.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 8.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Build the walls. With the floor in place you can start building the walls. These are often made from cinder block or poured concrete. You will want to discuss the benefits of each with your contractor. Make sure that the walls are constructed with the tops even and at a reasonable level to the surrounding ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9 Version 3.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 9.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 9.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Finish the walls. Pools need some type of moisture barrier, to keep all of the water from simply leeching out. There are lots of different options so talk to your contractor about what's right for you. The most common options include:<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10 Version 2.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#*A basic plastic liner",
                    "#*A real tile covering",
                    "#*A plaster coating",
                    "#*A sprayed plastic coating",
                    "#Fill in the outside. Fill in the outside area behind the walls of the pool. This filling material will vary depending on where you live. Your contractor should be able to advise on the cheapest vs. safest options.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 11.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 11-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 11 Version 2.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 11.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 11.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "#Fill the pool. With the pool complete, you'll just need to fill it up! Depending on how you had the pool plumbed, you can fill it from your own water supplies or you can have a water supply company come in and fill the pool. Enjoy!<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12 Version 2.jpg|gif=Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.360p.gif|giffirst=Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.360p.first.gif}}",
                    "*Keep your pool clean.",
                    "*Remove all the leaves and twigs that fall into your pool daily.",
                    "*Get a swimming teacher if you or your kids can't swim.",
                    "*Don't forget to drain the pool before winter if you don't want a skating rink.",
                    "*Do not go swimming in thunderstorms.",
                    "*Remember to find out the state regulations for home pools, as many places have requirements for barriers around the pool.",
                    "*Do your research BEFORE installation",
                    "*Be prepared to spend a lot of cash!",
                    "*Do not run around the pool.",
                    "<!-- Quick Summary code below. Please move/edit via section Edit link if needed. -->",
                    "== Quick Summary ==",
                    "{{Summary:Build-a-Swimming-Pool}}"
                ]
            }
        },
