        {
            "id": "15251535",
            "user": "ElaIP",
            "timestamp": "22:36, 2 September 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,075 bytes)",
            "change": 1,
            "summary": "(Fixing misspellings via the Spellchecker tool)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Build the walls. With the floor in place you can start building the walls. These are often made from cinder block or poured concrete. You will want to discuss the benefits of each with your contractor. Make sure that the walls are constructed with the tops even and at a reasonable level to the surrounding ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9 Version 3.jpg}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Build the walls. With the floor in place you can start building the walls. These are often made from cinderblock or poured concrete. You will want to discuss the benefits of each with your contractor. Make sure that the walls are constructed with the tops even and at a reasonable level to the surrounding ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9 Version 3.jpg}}"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "14518406",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "17:55, 12 August 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,074 bytes)",
            "change": 828,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "reverted_by": "Christy17",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Design the pool. You'll want to start by designing the pool that you want to build. Will it be round? Square? How deep will it be? What color do you want the pool to be? The more complex the design, the higher the cost.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Get a permit. Get a contractor lined up and apply for a permit with your local city. You might also have to apply with your local home owner's association, as not all neighborhoods allow pools. Your contractor may be able to do both of these things for you, if they are an experienced and qualified service.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 2 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Excavate the area. Rent the necessary construction equipment (usually at least a backhoe) and dig out the area where the pool will go. It is important to have your permit from the city at this point, as you don't want to risk running in to utility lines you didn't know about.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 3 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Grade the ground. You'll want to even out the ground as much as possible along what will become the bottom of the pool. This will make building the walls and putting in the floor much easier. There are many ways to grade the ground but if you are using a sloping floor, you'll definitely want someone with experience to do this.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Frame the walls. With the hole excavated and the land graded, you're ready to start putting in the walls. Start by framing the walls with wood and metal rebar. Make sure to keep the walls even and in line with each other.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 5 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Put in the plumbing. Get a licensed plumber to come in and add in the necessary plumbing for the pool. You will need to create a supply and filtration system which meets the codes for your area. Only a plumber with pool experience should be hired as an inexperienced one can ruin your pool.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 6 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Run the electricity. Get an electrician to run power to your pool if it is needed for lights or the filtration system. This is another time where it is important to hire a professional, as you can imagine what bad wiring plus water leads to.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 7 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Pour the floor. With the plumbing and electricity roughed in, you can start building the actual walls and floor. Get a cement truck and pour the floor, grading and smoothing once it is poured. Make sure to pay attention to angle of the floor if making a pool with depth variation.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 8 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Build the walls. With the floor in place you can start building the walls. These are often made from cinderblock or poured concrete. You will want to discuss the benefits of each with your contractor. Make sure that the walls are constructed with the tops even and at a reasonable level to the surrounding ground.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 9 Version 3.jpg}}",
                    "#Finish the walls. Pools need some type of moisture barrier, to keep all of the water from simply leeching out. There are lots of different options so talk to your contractor about what's right for you. The most common options include:<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 10 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Fill in the outside. Fill in the outside area behind the walls of the pool. This filling material will vary depending on where you live. Your contractor should be able to advise on the cheapest vs. safest options.<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 11.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 11-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 11 Version 2.jpg}}",
                    "#Fill the pool. With the pool complete, you'll just need to fill it up! Depending on how you had the pool plumbed, you can fill it from your own water supplies or you can have a water supply company come in and fill the pool. Enjoy!<br><br>{{whvid|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.360p.mp4|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12-preview.jpg|Build a Swimming Pool Step 12 Version 2.jpg}}"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Design the pool. You'll want to start by designing the pool that you want to build. Will it be round? Square? How deep will it be? What color do you want the pool to be? The more complex the design, the higher the cost.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Get a permit. Get a contractor lined up and apply for a permit with your local city. You might also have to apply with your local home owner's association, as not all neighborhoods allow pools. Your contractor may be able to do both of these things for you, if they are an experienced and qualified service.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Excavate the area. Rent the necessary construction equipment (usually at least a backhoe) and dig out the area where the pool will go. It is important to have your permit from the city at this point, as you don't want to risk running in to utility lines you didn't know about.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Grade the ground. You'll want to even out the ground as much as possible along what will become the bottom of the pool. This will make building the walls and putting in the floor much easier. There are many ways to grade the ground but if you are using a sloping floor, you'll definitely want someone with experience to do this.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Frame the walls. With the hole excavated and the land graded, you're ready to start putting in the walls. Start by framing the walls with wood and metal rebar. Make sure to keep the walls even and in line with each other.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Put in the plumbing. Get a licensed plumber to come in and add in the necessary plumbing for the pool. You will need to create a supply and filtration system which meets the codes for your area. Only a plumber with pool experience should be hired as an inexperienced one can ruin your pool.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Run the electricity. Get an electrician to run power to your pool if it is needed for lights or the filtration system. This is another time where it is important to hire a professional, as you can imagine what bad wiring plus water leads to.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Pour the floor. With the plumbing and electricity roughed in, you can start building the actual walls and floor. Get a cement truck and pour the floor, grading and smoothing once it is poured. Make sure to pay attention to angle of the floor if making a pool with depth variation.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Build the walls. With the floor in place you can start building the walls. These are often made from cinderblock or poured concrete. You will want to discuss the benefits of each with your contractor. Make sure that the walls are constructed with the tops even and at a reasonable level to the surrounding ground.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Finish the walls. Pools need some type of moisture barrier, to keep all of the water from simply leeching out. There are lots of different options so talk to your contractor about what's right for you. The most common options include:<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Fill in the outside. Fill in the outside area behind the walls of the pool. This filling material will vary depending on where you live. Your contractor should be able to advise on the cheapest vs. safest options.<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Fill the pool. With the pool complete, you'll just need to fill it up! Depending on how you had the pool plumbed, you can fill it from your own water supplies or you can have a water supply company come in and fill the pool. Enjoy!<br><br>[[Image:Build a Swimming Pool Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
